      "latency_ms": 2198.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 558,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through chemical, physical, and ecological lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The oceans, rivers, rain, and the water coming out of your tap.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Humidity in the air, fog, and steam from a boiling kettle.\n\nWater also has several fascinating physical properties:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its electrical polarity (one side is slightly positive, the other slightly negative), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries vital minerals and nutrients through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on water, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological Role\n* **For the Planet:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It drives global weather patterns, ocean currents, and the water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation).\n* **For Life:** All known forms of life depend on water. The human body is made of roughly **60% water**. It acts as a cushion for organs, regulates internal temperature, flushes out waste, and serves as the medium for almost all cellular chemical reactions.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple combination of two gases ($\\text{H}_2$ and $\\text{O}$) that magically forms a life-giving liquid at room temperature. Without it, life as we know it would not exist."
